    "main_topic": [
      {
        "h_title": "Nam Prik Noom (Thai Green Chilli Paste)",
        "main_title": "Nam Prik Noom (Thai Green Chilli Paste)",
        "author": null,
        "language": "en",
        "level": 1,
        "primary_content": [
          {
            "text": "Let’s make one of Thailand’s much loved but lesser known Nam Priks (chilli condiment), Nam Prik Noom (Thai Green Chilli Sauce).",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Estimated reading time: 3 minutes",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "South East Asia has a gamut of chilli based condiments & sauces under various guises and names! In Thailand alone, there are over a dozen well known ones and goodness knows how many more family recipes there are!",
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southeast_Asia",
            "urls": [
              {
                "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southeast_Asia",
                "anchor_text": "South East Asia"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "We’ll start this mini series of Thai chilli sauces/pastes with a slightly less well known one – Nam Prik Noom (Thai Green Chilli Sauce). Traditionally made with just some green chillies, shallots, garlic & the usual fish sauce, palm sugar & lime juice this is the personification of hot, sour & sweet.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "The recipe I make was learnt on one of my dive trips to Thailand. The lady who did all the cooking, adds some eggplant & tomatoes into the dip, the way her mother used to make it, apparently. And that’s how I’ve been making it all these years.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "We roast all the vegetables prior to chopping or you can also fry everything in a wok, in a little oil – I prefer roasting. What I like to do is place the aubergine on a direct low flame for about 10 minutes, while I get everything ready. It then goes under the grill with everything else. This not only give the aubergine a head start which it needs but also creates a slightly smoky flavour, àla Baba Ghanoush.",
            "url": "http://linsfood.com/baba-ghanoush/",
            "urls": [
              {
                "url": "http://linsfood.com/baba-ghanoush/",
                "anchor_text": "Baba Ghanoush"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "Cut the non pointy end of the shallots & garlic, this way, you can just squeeze out the middle after roasting.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "The chillies – I like to mix large non spicy varieties like jalapeños or any other nondescript ones you can find with 2-3 tiny Thai ones for the heat. This way, I’m getting the flavour without debilitating heat!",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Keeps in the fridge for about 2 weeks.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Let’s get our aprons on!",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "If you like the recipe, don’t forget to leave me a comment and that all important, 5-star rating! Thank you!",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "And if you make the recipe, share it on any platform and tag me @azlinbloor, and hashtag it #linsfood",
            "url": "https://www.instagram.com/azlinbloor/",
            "urls": [
              {
                "url": "https://www.instagram.com/azlinbloor/",
                "anchor_text": "@azlinbloor"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "Lin xx",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          }
        ],
